From: OmacVM
Subject: [PATCH] venus: the ring thread looks for the next command a little longer (macOS)

What: on macOS the Venus ring thread keeps looking for the guest's next
command for 200 us after the last one (yielding the CPU between looks)
before it starts upstream's naps (10, 20, 40 us ...). The ring still goes
idle after the guest's idle timeout (1 ms in Mesa's Venus driver), so a VM
that does no Vulkan work costs the same. OMACVM_VENUS_RING_SPIN_US sets the
time (at most 1 s; 0: upstream's 16 yields, then naps); it is read once per
process (call_once, no shared state between ring threads) and logged
("vkr: OMACVM_VENUS_RING_SPIN_US: ...", in qemu.log) when set.
Why: those naps end late on macOS (nanosleep's 10 us take 16, 160 take 243
on a Mac mini M4, macOS 27), and every late wake-up delays the guest's next
GPU job. A program that submits and waits (a Vulkan compute loop, OpenCL's
clFinish through rusticl on Zink, llama.cpp per graph) pays it every time.
Numbers (Mac mini M4, macOS 27.0, VM 4 CPU / 6 GB, Venus on KosmicKrisp, one
dispatch submit + wait, medians of 3 runs of 2000; starts in A B A B order,
nothing else running on the Mac): Vulkan submit + wait 185 / 185 ->
170 / 170 us, with the fence polled 166 / 165 -> 151 / 152 us, OpenCL kernel +
clFinish 229 / 229 -> 209 / 208 us, 64 dispatches recorded + submit + wait
366 / 365 -> 358 / 354 us; llama.cpp b11760 (Llama 3.2 3B Q4_K_M) pp512
156 / 158 -> 160 / 162 t/s, tg128 45.0 / 44.9 -> 45.3 / 45.4 t/s. QEMU's CPU
time during those runs +10 % (18.2 / 17.2 s -> 19.6 / 19.8 s). With the VM's
desktop idle (MacBook Pro M4 Max, A B A B, 120 s each) QEMU used 2.04 / 2.42 s
-> 2.29 / 2.59 s of CPU, rising with every start: no difference beyond that
drift (at most 0.2 s in 2 minutes).
Upstream: macOS-specific (the naps are fine with Linux's timers).
Test: src/bench/gpu (vklaunch, cllaunch) and llama-bench as above.

--- a/src/venus/vkr_ring.c
+++ b/src/venus/vkr_ring.c
@@ -6,6 +6,7 @@
 #include "vkr_ring.h"
 
 #include <stdio.h>
+#include <stdlib.h>
 #include <sys/resource.h>
 #include <time.h>
 
@@ -207,6 +208,34 @@ vkr_ring_relax(uint32_t *iter)
    clock_nanosleep(CLOCK_MONOTONIC, 0, &ts, NULL);
 }
 
+#ifdef __APPLE__
+/* OmacVM: for this long after a command the ring thread keeps looking for the
+ * guest's next one (yielding between looks) before it starts to sleep. Its
+ * naps of 10, 20, 40 us ... end late on macOS (10 us take 16), and the
+ * guest's next GPU job waits for the wake-up. 200 us covers the gap between a
+ * job and the next one in a submit-and-wait loop. The ring still goes idle
+ * (no CPU) after the guest's idle timeout, so an idle VM costs the same.
+ * OMACVM_VENUS_RING_SPIN_US sets it (0: upstream's 16 yields, then naps); it
+ * is read once per process (call_once) and logged when set. */
+static uint64_t vkr_ring_spin_ns = 200 * 1000;
+static once_flag vkr_ring_spin_once = ONCE_FLAG_INIT;
+
+static void
+vkr_ring_spin_init(void)
+{
+   const char *v = getenv("OMACVM_VENUS_RING_SPIN_US");
+   if (!v || !*v)
+      return;
+   uint64_t us = strtoull(v, NULL, 10);
+   if (us > 1000000)
+      us = 1000000;
+   vkr_ring_spin_ns = us * 1000;
+   vkr_log("OMACVM_VENUS_RING_SPIN_US: the ring thread looks for the next command for %" PRIu64
+           " us before it naps",
+           us);
+}
+#endif
+
 static bool
 vkr_ring_submit_cmd(struct vkr_ring *ring,
                     const uint8_t *buffer,
@@ -259,6 +288,10 @@ vkr_ring_thread(void *arg)
 #endif
    }
 
+#ifdef __APPLE__
+   call_once(&vkr_ring_spin_once, vkr_ring_spin_init);
+   const uint64_t spin_ns = vkr_ring_spin_ns;
+#endif
    uint64_t last_submit = vkr_ring_now();
    uint32_t relax_iter = 0;
    int ret = 0;
@@ -324,6 +357,12 @@ vkr_ring_thread(void *arg)
             }
          }
 
+#ifdef __APPLE__
+         if (vkr_ring_now() - last_submit < spin_ns) {
+            thrd_yield();
+            continue;
+         }
+#endif
          vkr_ring_relax(&relax_iter);
       }
    }
